<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Plácido de Castro</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1873–1908</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as José Plácido de Castro</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Plácido de Castro was a Brazilian soldier and surveyor who led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> tappers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre-war">Acrean Revolution</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a> in 1902–1903. His capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-alonso">Puerto Alonso</a> prepared the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-petropolis">Treaty of Petrópolis</a> of 1903, by which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> acquired Acre. He later governed part of the territory and was murdered in an ambush in 1908.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/placido-de-castro.jpg" alt="Plácido de Castro" width="320" height="571" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Torimem · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jos%C3%A9_Pl%C3%A1cido_de_Castro.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
